/* Exam 3 "Hear the why": spoken explanations for q_TBL3.js, q_GRG3.js and q_RMK3.js. Loaded after narration3.js. */
Object.assign(NARR_Q, {
"TBL3-001": "Acetaminophen poisoning shows nausea and vomiting with the clinical signs of liver failure, and acetaminophen levels can be measured in the blood. The liver signs come from N A P Q I, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen, a free radical that damages the liver. In class she said some drugs are so common in overdose, and so easy to detect in the blood, that most emergency rooms check for them, and acetaminophen is one. The trap is the other rows that also list a blood level. Benzodiazepines show central nervous system depression with relatively normal vital signs, and digoxin shows bradycardia, arrhythmias and high serum potassium. Tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth and agitation are tricyclic antidepressant signs, and that row lists no blood level.",
"TBL3-002": "The acetaminophen antidote is N-acetylcysteine. Acetaminophen is metabolized to N A P Q I, a free radical that damages the liver, and glutathione conjugates N A P Q I to clear it. In an overdose the glutathione runs short. In class she said N-acetylcysteine is given as a drug, and it gives the system ingredients to create more glutathione. The trap is sodium thiosulfate, which also enhances metabolism, but for cyanide, by supplying sulfur to the body's own enzyme rhodanese. Fomepizole is for the toxic alcohols, and deferoxamine is for iron.",
"TBL3-003": "N-acetylcysteine works by replenishing glutathione. Glutathione conjugates N A P Q I, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen. In an overdose the glutathione runs out, and N-acetylcysteine supplies the ingredients to make more, so N A P Q I keeps being conjugated. She classed this as enhancing metabolism by giving a cofactor. The trap is enzyme induction, which takes too much time to help in a poisoning. Inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase belongs to ethanol and fomepizole, and chelating ferric iron belongs to deferoxamine.",
"TBL3-004": "N-acetylcysteine loses effectiveness about eight hours after an acetaminophen overdose. It works by replenishing the glutathione that conjugates N A P Q I, and the acetaminophen special consideration puts the time point at about eight hours. The same entry adds that the antidote is still given after that point, because losing effectiveness is not the same as having no effect. The trap is borrowing another time from the course. About two hours belongs to single-dose activated charcoal: in class she said up to two hours, where it used to be one hour, and her quiz key says less than sixty minutes. Twenty four hours is the out-of-hospital window for modified-release verapamil, and no forty eight hour point appears in this row.",
"TBL3-005": "Yes, N-acetylcysteine is still given at twelve hours. The acetaminophen special consideration has two parts: the antidote loses effectiveness about eight hours after the overdose, and it should still be given past that time point. Less effective does not mean useless, because it still replenishes the glutathione that conjugates N A P Q I. The trap is reading eight hours as a cutoff after which the antidote stops working. Fomepizole inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and is the toxic alcohol antidote. Supportive care in place of an antidote is the salicylate and tricyclic antidepressant approach, not the acetaminophen answer.",
"TBL3-006": "Methanol's active metabolites cause anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity. Alcohol dehydrogenase turns methanol into formaldehyde, and formaldehyde dehydrogenase turns that into formic acid. The metabolites, not methanol itself, do the harm. She said formic acid will cause damage, especially to the eyes, to the retina of the eyes; it kills the receptors in the back of the retina, and sight is lost. The trap is the other toxic alcohol: ethylene glycol metabolites crystallize in the microvasculature and harm the kidney. Respiratory alkalosis followed by metabolic acidosis is salicylate poisoning, and high serum potassium with arrhythmias is digoxin.",
"TBL3-007": "Ethylene glycol's active metabolites can crystallize in the microvasculature, and renal toxicity is common. Ethylene glycol is metabolized through glycolic acid to oxalic acid. She said the oxalic acid can form little crystals that block blood flow in the small vessels and damage organs such as the liver and the kidneys. Her key adds that renal toxicity is common. The trap is methanol, the other toxic alcohol, whose metabolites cause anion-gap metabolic acidosis and damage to the eyes. Liver failure after nausea and vomiting is acetaminophen, and hyperthermia, confusion and seizures are salicylates.",
"TBL3-008": "The toxic alcohol antidotes are ethanol or fomepizole. Methanol and ethylene glycol do their harm only after alcohol dehydrogenase starts converting them, methanol on to formic acid and ethylene glycol on to oxalic acid. Both pathways start with that same enzyme, so one antidote pair serves both poisons. Ethanol outcompetes the enzyme, and fomepizole binds to and inhibits it. This is the treatment that inhibits bioactivation. The trap is another antidote pair: atropine and pralidoxime are for organophosphates, and naloxone and flumazenil are for opioids and benzodiazepines.",
"TBL3-009": "Ethanol and fomepizole both work by inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase. That enzyme starts the conversion of methanol to formic acid and of ethylene glycol to oxalic acid, and those metabolites cause the harm. She said you can give ethanol and outcompete the alcohol dehydrogenase metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol, while fomepizole will bind to and inhibit the enzyme. The trap is another mechanism from the table. Replenishing glutathione is N-acetylcysteine, renal ion trapping is sodium bicarbonate for salicylates, and chelating ferric iron is deferoxamine.",
"TBL3-010": "Fomepizole has the advantage. It is a stronger inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase than ethanol, and it has fewer adverse effects. Both treatments block the same enzyme, so the difference is in strength and in side effects. In class she described emergency rooms that once kept Everclear on hand to give a shot or two of ethanol, and said fomepizole gives a little bit better control of the dosing. The traps reverse or split the answer. Ethanol is not the stronger inhibitor, fomepizole does not carry more adverse effects, and the two are not equal.",
"TBL3-011": "Benzodiazepine overdose shows central nervous system depression with relatively normal vital signs, and benzodiazepine levels can be measured in the blood. The relatively normal vitals separate it from opioid overdose, where heart and breathing rates fall. The trap is another row that lists a blood level. Acetaminophen pairs the blood level with nausea, vomiting and signs of liver failure, and digoxin pairs it with bradycardia, arrhythmias and high serum potassium. Acetylcholine excess is organophosphate poisoning, and that row lists no blood level.",
"TBL3-012": "The benzodiazepine antidote is flumazenil. In the overdose the benzodiazepine acts as an agonist at its receptor, and flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at that same receptor, so it blocks the overdosed drug's effect. In class she gave flumazenil as an example of a treatment that is a receptor antagonist, used commonly for benzodiazepine overdoses. The trap is naloxone, the parallel antagonist for opioids. Atropine appears in the beta blocker and organophosphate rows. Physostigmine is not in the table; it was her example of a treatment that acts like a receptor agonist, for belladonna alkaloids.",
"TBL3-013": "Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor. In the overdose the benzodiazepine is the agonist, and flumazenil competes with it for the same receptor and blocks it. She settled on this wording: the benzos are the overdose, flumazenil is your antidote, and your treatment is the antagonist. The trap is calling flumazenil an agonist, when the benzodiazepine is the agonist. A competitive antagonist of opioid receptors is naloxone, and a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist is atropine in the organophosphate row.",
"TBL3-014": "The concern with flumazenil is withdrawal in a patient who is dependent on benzodiazepines. Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist that blocks the benzodiazepine receptor the overdosed drug is acting on. That reverses the overdose, but in a dependent patient, blocking the drug's action can bring on withdrawal symptoms. Naloxone carries the same concern in chronic opioid users. The trap is a special consideration from another row. Losing effectiveness at about eight hours is N-acetylcysteine, misleading serum levels belong to the digoxin antibody fragments, and a weaker inhibitor with more adverse effects describes ethanol compared with fomepizole.",
"TBL3-015": "Beta-blocker overdose shows hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status and respiratory depression. The blood pressure drops and the heart slows. The altered mental status separates it from calcium channel blocker overdose, which shares the hypotension and bradycardia but where the patient is often alert. The trap is that alert picture. Bradycardia with arrhythmias, high serum potassium and nausea is digoxin. Somnolence and coma with decreased heart and breathing rates and decreased pupils is opioid overdose.",
"TBL3-016": "Glucagon, vasopressors and intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion are all listed for beta-blocker overdose. The overdose leaves a slow heart and low blood pressure, and the six antidotes work against that from different sides. Atropine raises heart rate. Glucagon raises heart rate through cardiac cyclic A M P. Calcium is driven into cardiac cells. Vasopressors raise blood pressure. The emulsion acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough. H I E T has inotropic effects by mechanisms not understood. The calcium channel blocker row repeats the same six. Octreotide is wrong because it is the sulfonylurea and meglitinide antidote, and naloxone is wrong because it is the opioid antidote.",
"TBL3-017": "In beta-blocker overdose, atropine is listed to increase heart rate. The overdose causes bradycardia, and atropine counters it. Each of the six beta-blocker antidotes has its own entry. Calcium is given to try to drive calcium into cardiac cells, vasopressors increase blood pressure, and the fatty acid emulsion acts as a sink for a lipophilic drug. The trap is mixing these entries up. Atropine's entry reads differently in the organophosphate row, where it is listed as a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist.",
"TBL3-018": "Calcium is given to try to drive calcium into cardiac cells. A calcium channel blocker, as the name says, blocks calcium channels, and giving calcium tries to push more calcium into the heart cells. Beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdoses both leave the patient hypotensive and bradycardic, and calcium is listed for both. The trap is another cardiac mechanism. Raising cardiac cyclic A M P is glucagon, acting as a sink for a lipophilic drug is the fatty acid emulsion, and counteracting the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels is sodium bicarbonate.",
"TBL3-019": "In beta-blocker overdose, glucagon increases cardiac cellular cyclic A M P, and that increases heart rate. The overdose slows the heart, and glucagon counters the bradycardia. Atropine reaches the same goal, a faster heart rate, by its own route. In this table glucagon is listed for its effect on the heart, not for blood sugar. The trap is octreotide, which inhibits insulin release from the pancreas in sulfonylurea overdose. Driving calcium into cardiac cells is calcium, and counteracting sodium channel inhibition is sodium bicarbonate.",
"TBL3-020": "For H I E T, the mechanisms of its inotropic effects are not understood. It is listed among the beta-blocker antidotes, and in the repeated calcium channel blocker list, for its inotropic effects. It is the only one of the six without a stated mechanism. Each of the others has one. Atropine increases heart rate, glucagon increases cardiac cyclic A M P to increase heart rate, calcium is driven into cardiac cells, vasopressors increase blood pressure, and the fatty acid emulsion acts as a sink for a lipophilic drug. The trap is giving H I E T one of those mechanisms.",
"TBL3-021": "In beta-blocker overdose, vasopressors are listed to increase blood pressure. The overdose causes hypotension, and vasopressors counter it. The same entry applies to calcium channel blocker overdose, since that row repeats the beta-blocker list. In the tricyclic antidepressant row, dopamine or norepinephrine serve the same purpose, as supportive care to maintain blood pressure. The trap is another cardiac entry. Increasing heart rate is atropine, glucagon raises heart rate through cyclic A M P, and driving calcium into cardiac cells is calcium.",
"TBL3-022": "Intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough. The emulsion can take up the drug only if the drug is lipophilic enough to move into it, and then it holds the drug there. The condition is part of the mechanism. The trap is another way of removing or countering a drug. Binding a drug in an antibody complex that is renally eliminated is the digoxin antibody fragments. Raising cardiac cyclic A M P is glucagon, and raising blood pressure is the vasopressors.",
"TBL3-024": "Altered mental status in beta-blocker overdose distinguishes it from calcium channel blocker overdose. Both overdoses cause hypotension and bradycardia, and the antidotes are the same. The beta-blocker patient may have altered mental status, while the calcium channel blocker patient is often alert. The trap is choosing a finding the two rows share. Bradycardia and hypotension appear in both rows, so neither can tell them apart. The other trap reverses the rows; the calcium channel blocker row says often alert, not altered mental status.",
"TBL3-025": "Calcium channel blocker overdose shows hypotension and bradycardia, but the patient is often alert. Blood pressure drops and the heart slows, just as in beta-blocker overdose. The alertness separates the two, because beta-blocker overdose adds altered mental status and respiratory depression. The trap is that beta-blocker picture. Somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breathing rates and decreased pupils are opioid overdose. Tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation and seizures are tricyclic antidepressant overdose.",
"TBL3-026": "The antidotes and mechanisms for calcium channel blocker overdose are the same as for beta-blocker overdose. Both overdoses produce hypotension and bradycardia, and the calcium channel blocker row reads same as above for beta-blocker overdose in both the antidote and the mechanism columns. The six are atropine, calcium, glucagon, H I E T, vasopressors and intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion, glucagon included. Only the symptoms differ: the patient is often alert. Antibody fragments are the digoxin antidote, and sodium bicarbonate with fluids is the tricyclic antidepressant treatment.",
"TBL3-027": "Atropine, calcium and H I E T are all listed for calcium channel blocker overdose. The calcium channel blocker row repeats the beta-blocker list: atropine, calcium, glucagon, H I E T, vasopressors and intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion. Atropine increases heart rate against the bradycardia, calcium tries to drive calcium into cardiac cells, and H I E T has inotropic effects by mechanisms not understood. The trap is pralidoxime, which is paired with atropine for organophosphates and removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase. Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote.",
"TBL3-028": "Digoxin overdose shows bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, and nausea and vomiting, and digoxin levels can be measured in the blood. Elevated serum potassium is the finding no other row lists. After the antibody fragments are given, the measured total rises and no longer guides more doses. The trap is another row with a blood level. Acetaminophen pairs it with nausea, vomiting and signs of liver failure, and benzodiazepines pair it with central nervous system depression and relatively normal vitals. The tricyclic antidepressant row lists no blood level.",
"TBL3-029": "The digoxin antidote is digoxin specific antibody fragments, called Fab. The fragments bind digoxin in the blood so it can no longer act, and the bound drug is renally eliminated. This is the example of inactivation by an antibody. Digoxin immune Fab is ovine; the fragments come from the serum of sheep. She called it one form of antibody made to grab digoxin that somebody has overdosed on; it grabs it and nicely clears it out of the blood. The trap is the fatty acid emulsion, a sink for a lipophilic drug in beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose. Deferoxamine is for iron, and atropine with pralidoxime is for organophosphates.",
"TBL3-030": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments bind digoxin, and the bound digoxin is renally eliminated. This is inactivation, not action at a receptor. An inactivator binds the toxin so it cannot reach its target, and the complex is then cleared. She said the antibody grabs the digoxin and nicely clears it out of the blood. The trap is renal ion trapping. The complex does leave through the kidney, but by binding, not by ion trapping; ion trapping is sodium bicarbonate for salicylates. Chelating ferric iron is deferoxamine, and increasing cardiac cyclic A M P is glucagon.",
"TBL3-031": "After digoxin antibody fragments are given, the measured total digoxin, free plus bound to the fragments, is increased. The fragments bind digoxin in the blood, and the bound digoxin is still there to be measured. So the serum level cannot be used to calculate more doses of the fragments. The trap is expecting the level to fall. The level does not read zero, the fragments do not hide digoxin from the test, and although the complex is renally eliminated, the problem named is that the measured total goes up.",
"TBL3-032": "Dilated pupils, dry mouth and an elongated Q R S interval are all listed for tricyclic antidepressant overdose. The row has two parts. First, a fast heart, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation and seizures. Second, cardiac effects: an elongated Q R S interval and reduced contractility, sometimes with vasodilation. Sodium bicarbonate is given for the elongated Q R S interval because it counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels. The traps are opposites. The row lists tachycardia, not bradycardia, and decreased pupils belong to opioid overdose.",
"TBL3-033": "Intravenous fluids, dopamine or norepinephrine, and sodium bicarbonate for an elongated Q R S interval are the three treatments listed for tricyclic antidepressant overdose. The fluids and the dopamine or norepinephrine are supportive care to maintain blood pressure. The bicarbonate counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels. The trap is glucagon, which is on the beta-blocker list to raise a slow heart rate, while tricyclic antidepressant overdose causes tachycardia. Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote and is not in this row.",
"TBL3-034": "In tricyclic antidepressant overdose, intravenous fluids with dopamine or norepinephrine are supportive care to maintain blood pressure, and sodium bicarbonate counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels. The overdose reduces contractility and sometimes causes vasodilation, which bears on blood pressure, and it elongates the Q R S interval. Each treatment answers one of those problems. The trap is renal ion trapping, which is what sodium bicarbonate does for salicylates, not here. Chelation is for metals such as iron, and replenishing glutathione is N-acetylcysteine.",
"TBL3-035": "Sodium bicarbonate has two mechanisms in the table. For tricyclic antidepressants, it counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels and is given for an elongated Q R S interval. For salicylates, it causes renal ion trapping to enhance elimination. She said salicylates and some barbiturates are mildly acidic, and a bag of sodium bicarbonate will make the urine a bit more basic. In basic urine the salicylate carries a charge, so it stays in the urine and is excreted. The trap is swapping the two, or giving both rows one mechanism. Ion trapping needs a weak acid like salicylate, and in tricyclic overdose the bicarbonate acts on the heart.",
"TBL3-036": "Opioid overdose shows somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breathing rates, and decreased pupils. The falling heart and breathing rates separate it from benzodiazepine overdose, where the central nervous system is depressed but the vitals stay relatively normal. The trap is the pupils. Dilated pupils belong to tricyclic antidepressant overdose, while opioid overdose decreases the pupils. Hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis, is salicylate poisoning. Central nervous system depression with normal vitals is the benzodiazepine row.",
"TBL3-037": "The opioid antidote is naloxone. The overdosed opioid acts as an agonist at the opioid receptor. She said naloxone is basically an antagonist of an opioid receptor, so it blocks the effect of the overdosed drug. It was her first example of a treatment that is a receptor antagonist. Naloxone carries one concern: in chronic opioid users it can trigger withdrawal. The trap is flumazenil, the parallel antagonist for benzodiazepines. Octreotide is for sulfonylureas and meglitinides, and atropine appears in the beta blocker and organophosphate rows.",
"TBL3-038": "Naloxone is a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors. The overdosed opioid is the agonist at the receptor, and naloxone competes with it for the same receptor and blocks it. She said naloxone is basically an antagonist of an opioid receptor. The trap is flumazenil, which does the same thing at the benzodiazepine receptor. A muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist is atropine in the organophosphate row. Binding a drug so that it is renally eliminated is the digoxin antibody fragments, not naloxone.",
"TBL3-039": "In chronic opioid users, naloxone can trigger withdrawal. Naloxone competitively blocks the opioid receptor. That reverses an overdose, but in someone who uses opioids chronically, cutting off the opioid's action at the receptor brings on withdrawal. Flumazenil carries the parallel concern in a patient dependent on benzodiazepines. The trap is a special consideration from another row. Losing effectiveness at about eight hours is N-acetylcysteine, a rising measured level is the digoxin antibody fragments, and more adverse effects describes ethanol compared with fomepizole.",
"TBL3-040": "Flumazenil and naloxone are the two antidotes that can cause withdrawal. Flumazenil can cause withdrawal symptoms in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient, and naloxone can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users. Both are competitive antagonists that block the receptor the overdosed drug is acting on, so in a dependent patient they take away the drug's action. The trap is pairing either one with an antidote whose concern is something else. N-acetylcysteine's concern is its eight hour time point, fomepizole's entry is its advantage over ethanol, and the antibody fragments' concern is the serum level.",
"TBL3-041": "Salicylate poisoning runs in order: hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis first, then metabolic acidosis. Hyperthermia, confusion and seizures are listed with it. Salicylate is mildly acidic, and that chemistry is also the basis of the treatment, since sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic and traps the charged salicylate for elimination. The trap is the order; metabolic acidosis does not come first. Anion-gap metabolic acidosis with damage to the eyes is methanol, and respiratory depression with altered mental status is beta-blocker overdose.",
"TBL3-042": "Besides the acid-base changes, salicylate poisoning lists hyperthermia, confusion and seizures. The acid-base sequence is hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis. The treatment uses the drug's chemistry: salicylate is mildly acidic, so intravenous sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic and traps the charged salicylate in the urine for elimination. The trap is a finding from another row. Damage to the eyes is methanol, elevated serum potassium with arrhythmias is digoxin, and somnolence, coma and decreased pupils are opioid overdose.",
"TBL3-043": "Salicylate poisoning is treated with supportive care and intravenous sodium bicarbonate. Salicylate is mildly acidic. She said the team will hang a bag of sodium bicarbonate on the patient, and that will make the urine a bit more basic. In basic urine the salicylate carries a charge, is not reabsorbed, and is excreted. This is renal ion trapping. The trap is the tricyclic antidepressant row, which lists intravenous fluids with dopamine or norepinephrine to maintain blood pressure. Ethanol or fomepizole is for the toxic alcohols, and octreotide is for sulfonylureas.",
"TBL3-044": "In salicylate poisoning, intravenous sodium bicarbonate works by renal ion trapping to enhance elimination. Salicylate is mildly acidic. Bicarbonate makes the urine more basic, so the salicylate in the urine carries a negative charge, and the charged molecule is not reabsorbed. It stays in the urine and is excreted. This is the treatment that enhances renal elimination. The trap is chelation, which is for metals such as iron, not for salicylate. Inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase is ethanol or fomepizole, and replenishing glutathione is N-acetylcysteine.",
"TBL3-045": "The one symptom listed for sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdose is hypoglycemia. The antidote, octreotide, inhibits insulin release from the pancreas, and holding back insulin release counters the low blood sugar. This row has no special considerations question. Glucagon also appears in the table, but in the beta-blocker row, for its effect on heart rate. The trap is a single finding from another row. Hyperthermia belongs to salicylates, elevated serum potassium belongs to digoxin, and ocular toxicity belongs to methanol.",
"TBL3-046": "The antidote for sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdose is octreotide. The overdose causes hypoglycemia. Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas, and holding back insulin release treats the low blood sugar. The trap is glucagon. Glucagon does appear in the table, but in the beta-blocker row, where it increases cardiac cyclic A M P to increase heart rate. Naloxone is the opioid antidote, and flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote; neither acts on insulin release.",
"TBL3-047": "Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas. Sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdose causes hypoglycemia, and holding back insulin release counters the low blood sugar. The row has no special considerations question. The trap is the glucagon entry. Increasing cardiac cellular cyclic A M P is glucagon in beta-blocker overdose, not octreotide. Replenishing glutathione is N-acetylcysteine for acetaminophen, and a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors is naloxone.",
"TBL3-049": "Vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea appear within one to six hours after an iron overdose. The gut symptoms come first. Within six to twenty four hours come lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting, bloody diarrhea, shock, hypotension and tachycardia. Liver dysfunction and failure are possible in two to five days. The trap is mixing up the three windows. Six to twenty four hours is the shock stage, two to five days is the liver stage, and about eight hours is the acetaminophen antidote's time point, not part of the iron row.",
"TBL3-050": "In iron overdose, liver dysfunction and failure are possible in two to five days. Liver involvement is the last stage of the iron timeline. Vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea come first, within one to six hours. Lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting and diarrhea, shock, hypotension and tachycardia come within six to twenty four hours. The trap is placing the liver in one of the earlier windows, or much later. The timeline ends with the liver at two to five days, not after two weeks.",
"TBL3-051": "The iron antidote is deferoxamine. Deferoxamine is a heavy metal chelator: it binds the circulating ferric iron so the iron cannot act, and the complex is cleared. In class she said two iron chelating agents have been on the market, deferoxamine and the oral deferasirox, and the table lists deferoxamine. The trap is another chelator. Dimercaprol chelates gold, arsenic, lead and mercury, and penicillamine chelates copper. Pralidoxime is an organophosphate antidote and is not a chelator.",
"TBL3-052": "Deferoxamine chelates the circulating ferric iron. A chelator binds a metal more tightly than the body's own macromolecules do. Deferoxamine binds the ferric iron in the blood, inactivating it, and the complex is cleared. It belongs to the inactivation approach to treating poisonings, and the iron row has no special considerations question. The trap is removing a poison from its target enzyme, which is pralidoxime for organophosphates. Renal ion trapping is sodium bicarbonate for salicylates, and inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase is ethanol or fomepizole.",
"TBL3-053": "Organophosphate insecticide poisoning causes acetylcholine excess, recalled as SLUDGE or DUMBELLS. SLUDGE stands for the muscarinic effects: salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, gastric cramping and emesis. Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine, so acetylcholine builds up. She said if you are exposed to organophosphate pesticides, you end up with too much acetylcholine in your synaptic cleft. The trap is the tricyclic antidepressant picture of tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth and agitation. Hypotension and bradycardia in an alert patient is calcium channel blocker overdose, and somnolence, coma and decreased pupils is opioid overdose.",
"TBL3-054": "Atropine and pralidoxime are the two organophosphate antidotes. Atropine is a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist, so it blocks the excess acetylcholine at those receptors. Pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase. She said pralidoxime is going to pull off the organophosphate and put it onto itself, and when it does that, it can be eliminated. The trap is physostigmine, which is not in the table. Physostigmine blocks acetylcholinesterase to increase acetylcholine, so it acts like a receptor agonist; it was her example for belladonna alkaloid exposure. Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote.",
"TBL3-055": "In organophosphate poisoning, atropine is a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist. The organophosphate inhibits acetylcholinesterase, acetylcholine builds up, and atropine blocks the muscarinic receptors so the excess cannot act there. She said that with organophosphate exposure you end up with too much acetylcholine in your synaptic cleft, and atropine is used to antagonize that. The trap is pralidoxime, which removes the organophosphate from the enzyme. Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase is what the organophosphate itself does, and the benzodiazepine receptor antagonist is flumazenil.",
"TBL3-056": "Pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase. The organophosphate binds the enzyme and inactivates it. Pralidoxime pulls the organophosphate off the enzyme onto itself, the complex can then be eliminated, and the enzyme is freed. This fails once the organophosphate has aged. She called it aging: part of some organophosphates falls off, and the rest is then permanently bound and cannot be pulled off. Whether it happens depends on the chemical structure of the organophosphate. The trap is atropine, the muscarinic receptor antagonist. Breaking down acetylcholine is what the freed enzyme does, and inhibiting acetylcholinesterase to raise acetylcholine is physostigmine.",
"TBL3-057": "Atropine's mechanism is written two ways in the table. In beta-blocker overdose it is listed by its effect, increasing heart rate, against the bradycardia of the overdose. In organophosphate poisoning it is listed by its action, a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist, against the acetylcholine excess. The trap is swapping the two rows. The other wrong pairings belong to other drugs: removing the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase is pralidoxime, and increasing cardiac cyclic A M P is glucagon.",
"TBL3-058": "Three rows list measuring blood levels: acetaminophen, benzodiazepines and digoxin. Each pairs the measurement with symptoms. Acetaminophen has nausea, vomiting and signs of liver failure. Benzodiazepines have central nervous system depression with relatively normal vitals. Digoxin has bradycardia, arrhythmias and high serum potassium, and its level stops being useful once the antibody fragments are given. The trap is a row with symptoms only. Tricyclic antidepressants list symptoms and the elongated Q R S interval, and organophosphates list acetylcholine excess, with no blood level.",
"GRG3-001": "Out of hospital, emesis is not induced, activated charcoal is considered if there are no contraindications, and transport to the hospital is not delayed to give charcoal. She said inducing vomiting at home is no longer recommended because it is too dangerous; the person might aspirate. Charcoal is something done when the patient gets to the hospital, so it never holds up transport. Each trap breaks one part. Inducing emesis at home is wrong, giving charcoal at home before calling for transport is wrong, and saying charcoal has no role is wrong, because it is still considered.",
"GRG3-002": "An asymptomatic patient found within six hours of an immediate-release calcium channel blocker ingestion goes to a hospital. Six hours is the immediate-release window, and inside it the drug may not all have been absorbed yet. She said that if they have not passed the six hour mark for an immediate-release exposure, they are still within the time when they could really badly respond. The trap is the answer for a patient found after six hours, a call to the Poison Center and probably care at home. The answer does not depend on an estimated dose; she added that if you cannot confirm how much was taken, the patient goes in. Waiting at home for symptoms is also wrong, because inside the window the patient goes now.",
"GRG3-003": "An asymptomatic patient found six hours after an immediate-release calcium channel blocker ingestion can probably be managed at home after a call to the Poison Center. An immediate-release product is absorbed rapidly, so by six hours it should have been absorbed and would have produced symptoms. She said that if they are not symptomatic after six hours post ingestion, they are going to be OK. The Poison Center informs you of the follow-up. The trap is the answer inside the window, getting them to a hospital. Driving them in the family car is wrong too, since any patient who does go in goes by ambulance, and charcoal is not given at home.",
"GRG3-004": "An asymptomatic patient found within eighteen hours of a modified-release calcium channel blocker, other than verapamil, goes to a hospital. Modified-release tablets tend to clump into bezoars that can break apart at unpredictable times, so the patient may still absorb a large dose. She said that if they ingested the modified-release product and you found them within eighteen hours, you need to get them in. The trap is the answer after the window, a call to the Poison Center and care at home. Eight hours is the sustained-release beta blocker window, not this one.",
"GRG3-005": "An asymptomatic patient found eighteen hours after a modified-release calcium channel blocker, other than verapamil, can probably be managed at home after a call to the Poison Center. The eighteen hour window covers the time in which a clump of tablets could still break apart and release the drug. Past it with no symptoms, that danger has passed, and the Poison Center informs you of the follow-up. The trap is the twenty four hour window, which applies only to modified-release verapamil. The call is still needed, and emesis is not induced.",
"GRG3-006": "An asymptomatic patient found within twenty four hours of modified-release verapamil goes to a hospital. Verapamil gets the longest window, twenty four hours, not the eighteen of the other modified-release calcium channel blockers. She said the reason is its mechanism: it actually has multiple mechanisms, and it is not quite as clean a calcium channel blocker as the other ones. The concern lasts while the clump of tablets could still break apart. The trap is applying eighteen hours, or the six hour immediate-release window, to verapamil. Waiting at home for symptoms is also wrong; inside the window the patient goes in.",
"GRG3-007": "An asymptomatic patient found twenty four hours after modified-release verapamil can probably be managed at home after a call to the Poison Center. Twenty four hours is the longest window, because verapamil has multiple mechanisms. Once it has passed with no symptoms, she said they are probably OK; you are going to call the poison center, but they are OK. The Poison Center informs you of the follow-up. The trap is a longer window such as forty eight hours, which does not exist here. Skipping the call is wrong, and charcoal is not given at home.",
"GRG3-008": "The windows differ because of the formulation. Immediate-release formulations are absorbed more rapidly and do not tend to form bezoars. Extended-release products tend to form bezoars, which may break apart at unpredictable times, so you cannot be sure when the patient will absorb the medication. She said the tablets often clump in the stomach or in the small intestine, and when the clump breaks apart there can be a huge absorption of the drug. The trap is a dose explanation; the issue is timing, not the amount per tablet. The other trap reverses the two formulations, and clearance by the kidney is not the reason; the answer is about absorption.",
"GRG3-009": "A patient who is symptomatic when found goes to a hospital. The time windows of six, eighteen and twenty four hours for calcium channel blockers sort only the patients who have no symptoms. Once symptoms are present, the time since ingestion and the formulation no longer change the answer, for either drug class. The trap is applying the asymptomatic rule, a call to the Poison Center and care at home, to a patient who already has symptoms. Waiting for the window to pass is wrong, and emesis is not induced.",
"GRG3-010": "A patient who needs to go to the hospital goes by ambulance. The answer is the same for calcium channel blockers and beta blockers. She said that if the patient starts crashing, the ambulance has what is needed to help them. In a family car stuck in traffic there are no intravenous lines to hang, no pressors and no paddles. The trap is splitting the answer by symptoms. An asymptomatic patient can still crash on the way, so the ambulance applies to any patient who needs transport, and the vehicle is not whichever one arrives first.",
"GRG3-011": "For calcium channel blockers, follow-up of an asymptomatic patient is determined by the judgment of the Poison Center, and no interval is named. She said that for the calcium channel blockers, follow-up will be determined based on the judgment of the poison center. The trap is the beta blocker wording, follow-up calls at appropriate intervals for up to twelve to twenty four hours, which belongs to the other guideline. No single call at six hours is named, and follow-up is not skipped for an asymptomatic patient.",
"GRG3-012": "When self-harm is suspected, the patient is referred to the emergency department, and then referred to mental health care. Treating the poisoning is not enough. She said they are going to need some help, some counseling, some evaluation, so that whatever is going on in their lives can get help. The emergency department treats the exposure first, and mental health care follows. The trap is stopping at one half. The emergency department alone leaves out mental health care, and mental health care alone leaves the overdose untreated.",
"GRG3-013": "For beta blockers, the three steps to reduce absorption out of hospital are the same as for calcium channel blockers. Do not induce emesis, because it is too dangerous; the person might aspirate. Consider activated charcoal if there are no contraindications; she said that based on patient outcomes, charcoal is probably the best option. Do not delay transport to the hospital to give it, because charcoal is a hospital step. She said people are not asked to drink a charcoal slurry or have it put down a nasogastric tube in the home. The traps are inducing emesis in an alert patient and giving charcoal at home through a tube.",
"GRG3-014": "An asymptomatic patient found within six hours of an immediate-release beta blocker, other than sotalol, goes to a hospital. The immediate-release window is six hours, the same as for calcium channel blockers. An immediate-release product is absorbed rapidly, but a patient still inside six hours may not have absorbed all of it and could respond badly. The trap is the answer for after the window, a call to the Poison Center and care at home. The twelve to twenty four hour interval is the follow-up schedule after the window, not the answer inside it.",
"GRG3-015": "An asymptomatic patient found six hours after an immediate-release beta blocker, other than sotalol, can probably be managed at home after a call to the Poison Center. By six hours an immediate-release product should have been absorbed and would have produced symptoms. She said that if they are not symptomatic after six hours post ingestion, they are going to be OK. The trap is the eight hour window, which applies to sustained-release beta blockers, not immediate-release ones. The Poison Center is still called and informs you of the follow-up.",
"GRG3-016": "An asymptomatic patient found within eight hours of a sustained-release beta blocker goes to a hospital. Sustained-release tablets tend to clump into bezoars that can break apart at unpredictable times, so the patient may still absorb a large dose. She said that for a sustained-release beta blocker the time frame is within eight hours, and you have to get them to the hospital. The trap is the six hour immediate-release window. The beta blocker window is also shorter than the eighteen hours for calcium channel blockers, a difference she tied to the mechanisms of the two classes.",
"GRG3-017": "An asymptomatic patient found eight hours after a sustained-release beta blocker can probably be managed at home after a call to the Poison Center. The eight hour window covers the time in which a clump of sustained-release tablets could still break apart. Past it with no symptoms, the patient is expected to be OK. The Poison Center informs you of the follow-up, which for beta blockers means calls for up to twelve to twenty four hours. The trap is the eighteen hour window, which belongs to modified-release calcium channel blockers. Charcoal is not given at home.",
"GRG3-018": "A sustained-release beta blocker carries the longer window because sustained-release products tend to form bezoars, which may break apart at unpredictable times. You cannot be sure when the patient will absorb the medication. Immediate-release formulations are absorbed more rapidly and do not tend to form bezoars. She said that extended-release and sustained-release tablets, because of their formulation, tend to stick together. The trap reverses the two formulations. Potency per milligram and charcoal binding do not set the window; the formulation does.",
"GRG3-019": "A patient with symptoms goes to a hospital, whatever the formulation and however long ago the drug was taken. The six hour window for an immediate-release beta blocker applies only to a patient who has no symptoms. This patient was found after six hours, but the symptoms decide the answer, not the clock. The trap is applying the asymptomatic rule, a call to the Poison Center and care at home. Watching at home is wrong, and waiting until eight hours confuses this case with the sustained-release window for asymptomatic patients.",
"GRG3-020": "The patient goes by ambulance. Beta blocker and calcium channel blocker overdoses can crash on the way, and the ambulance carries intravenous lines, pressors and paddles. She said that if you get stuck in traffic trying to get them to the hospital and they start to crash, you are out of luck: no intravenous lines to hang, no pressors to hang, no paddles. The trap is speed; a family car may arrive sooner but carries none of that. The window decides whether the patient goes in, not how, and the Poison Center does not pick the vehicle.",
"GRG3-021": "For beta blockers, follow-up calls are made to determine outcome at appropriate intervals for up to twelve to twenty four hours, based on the judgment of the poison center. The calcium channel blocker guideline leaves follow-up to the Poison Center's judgment with no interval named. She said the difference reflects what the data are showing for these different kinds of cases. The trap is that calcium channel blocker wording. Six hours is the immediate-release window for going to a hospital, not a follow-up schedule, and follow-up is by calls, not a hospital admission.",
"GRG3-022": "Suspected self-harm means referral to the emergency department, and then referral to mental health care. The answer is the same for beta blockers and calcium channel blockers. She said that as pharmacists we cannot stop with just our drug knowledge; we need to think about the whole person and be sure they are accessing other care they may need. The trap is stopping after the emergency department. Managing at home after six hours does not apply when self-harm is suspected, and a report to the Poison Center with no further step leaves out both referrals.",
"GRG3-023": "Sotalol is the drug excepted from the six hour rule for immediate-release beta blockers. Each guideline carries one named exception. For beta blockers, immediate-release products except sotalol follow the six hour window, and no separate window is given for sotalol. For calcium channel blockers, modified-release products except verapamil follow the eighteen hour window, and modified-release verapamil has twenty four hours. The trap is verapamil, which is the calcium channel blocker exception. Propranolol is not an exception; it appears in the course as a drug with a large volume of distribution. Saying no drug is excepted is wrong, because sotalol is named.",
"GRG3-025": "Three recommendations are the same for both classes: the steps to reduce absorption, getting a symptomatic patient to a hospital, and referring suspected self-harm to the emergency department and then to mental health care. The two guidelines differ in only two places, so these two are not the same. The extended-release window is eighteen hours, or twenty four for verapamil, for calcium channel blockers, against eight hours for sustained-release beta blockers. Follow-up is left to the Poison Center's judgment for calcium channel blockers, against calls for up to twelve to twenty four hours for beta blockers.",
"GRG3-026": "For calcium channel blockers, follow-up is determined by the judgment of the Poison Center. For beta blockers, follow-up calls are made at appropriate intervals for up to twelve to twenty four hours, also by the poison center's judgment. She said there are slightly different details in that follow-up just because of what the data are showing for these different kinds of cases. The trap is swapping the two; the twelve to twenty four hour interval belongs to beta blockers. Both guidelines include follow-up, and only one names an interval.",
"GRG3-027": "The difference between the beta blocker and calcium channel blocker windows comes from the mechanism of action of each class. Two reasons answer two different questions. Immediate-release against extended-release is explained by bezoars. She said the clumping of the tablets is the biggest issue, but the mechanism of action will speak to why there is a difference in time between the beta blockers and the calcium channel blockers. The trap is assuming beta blockers do not form bezoars; the bezoar reason applies to both. Both guidelines were published in the same year, two thousand five, and charcoal is considered for both.",
"GRG3-028": "Modified-release verapamil carries twenty four hours because of its mechanism. She said it actually has multiple mechanisms, and it is not quite as clean a calcium channel blocker as the other ones. So the concern lasts longer than the eighteen hours of the other modified-release calcium channel blockers. The trap is a bezoar answer. All extended-release products form bezoars, so bezoars explain why extended-release windows are longer, not why verapamil's is the longest. She did not call verapamil a beta blocker, and charcoal is considered for all of these ingestions.",
"GRG3-029": "At ten hours after extended-release verapamil, an asymptomatic patient goes to a hospital. The verapamil window is twenty four hours, so ten hours is still inside it, and the clump of tablets could still break apart and be absorbed. She said it is twenty four hours for verapamil extended release, a pretty long time frame, because it is possible that somebody could all of a sudden absorb that clump of medication. The trap is a shorter window. Eight hours is the sustained-release beta blocker window, and eighteen hours is for the other modified-release calcium channel blockers.",
"GRG3-030": "Inside the window, with an unconfirmed amount, call the Poison Center and probably bring the patient into the hospital. No symptoms inside the window does not mean a small dose, because the drug may not all have been absorbed yet. She said that if you cannot confirm they only took maybe one or two tablets, you probably need to get them into the hospital, because they have possibly not absorbed everything they ingested. The trap is reading the lack of symptoms as a small dose, or estimating the dose and observing at home. Waiting until the window passes is also wrong.",
"GRG3-031": "The sustained-release beta blocker has the shortest extended-release window, eight hours. The modified-release calcium channel blocker window, other than verapamil, is eighteen hours, and modified-release verapamil is twenty four hours, the longest. The immediate-release window is shared by both classes at six hours. She said that at eighteen hours for calcium channel blockers, but eight hours for beta blockers, you are still within the window that the clump of tablets could break apart. The trap is attaching eighteen hours to beta blockers, or eight hours to calcium channel blockers.",
"GRG3-032": "The asymptomatic patient found six hours after an immediate-release beta blocker can probably be managed at home after a call to the Poison Center. Two conditions must both hold: no symptoms, and found at or after the end of the window for that product. The windows are six hours for immediate-release, eight for sustained-release beta blockers, eighteen for modified-release calcium channel blockers, and twenty four for modified-release verapamil. If either condition fails, the patient goes to a hospital by ambulance. Each trap fails one condition: still inside the window, or already symptomatic.",
"GRG3-033": "Three factors decide what happens: how long after ingestion the person was found, whether they are symptomatic, and the formulation. She said there are things to think about regarding the time frame of when you found them, whether they are symptomatic or not, and the kind of formulation they were exposed to. The formulation sets the window, the time since ingestion places the patient inside or past it, and symptoms send the patient to a hospital at any time. The traps are charcoal at home, which is not given, and the family car, since transport is by ambulance.",
"GRG3-034": "Both out-of-hospital guidelines were written by a panel of the American Association of Poison Control Centers. Each is an evidence-based consensus guideline for out-of-hospital management, meant to help poison center staff triage and manage people who swallowed these drugs outside a hospital. She said the authors pulled together all the data from patients who overdosed on these different formulations and did the statistical analysis. The trap is a familiar agency. The Food and Drug Administration labels drugs, and neither guideline names the Centers for Disease Control or the heart associations.",
"GRG3-035": "Paul Wax is the first author of the beta blocker guideline, and Kent Olson is the first author of the calcium channel blocker guideline. The same twelve-member panel of the American Association of Poison Control Centers wrote both guidelines, and only the order of the names changes. Both men are on both author lists, which is why reversing them is the trap. Rodriguez-Mozaz and Weinberg wrote the meeting report on pharmaceuticals in water, and Marraffa, Cohen and Howland wrote the primer on antidotes.",
"RMK3-001": "Flushing is the source that other disposal methods of drugs could reduce. Flushing unused medications is improper disposal, a choice that can be changed. Her example was unused antibiotics dumped down the toilet, and she said improper disposal of medications is another source. The other two sources are sorted the same way, by whether the step can change. Animal waste could be reduced by controls on feedlots and farms. Human waste cannot be reduced, because people take medications. The trap is eating fish, which is a route of human exposure, not a source.",
"RMK3-002": "Animal waste could be reduced by controls on feedlots and farms. Livestock on feedlots and in chicken houses are sometimes given medications such as hormones and antibiotics, and their waste carries those drugs into water supplies. Controls where the animals are kept could cut that source. The trap is the fix for another source. Other disposal methods are the fix for flushing, and the statement that a source cannot be reduced belongs to human waste. Wild animals are not the source either; they are the ones exposed.",
"RMK3-003": "Human waste is the source that cannot be reduced, because people take medications. The parent drug and its metabolites leave the body in waste. She said we take medications to treat and prevent disease, and we are not ever going to stop that. So this source stays. The trap is one of the two sources that do have a fix. Flushing could be reduced by other disposal methods of drugs, and animal waste could be reduced by controls on feedlots and farms. Eating fish is a route of human exposure, not a source.",
"RMK3-004": "For aquatic wildlife, the sources are the same as for humans: flushing, animal waste and human waste. These sources reach the same water that humans and wildlife are both exposed to. She said those are the same sources for animals out in the wild, so the same source for humans and animals. Wastewater treatment plants, which do not remove all drugs, add to it for both. The trap is narrowing the wildlife sources to animal waste alone, or treating them as separate from human sources. The unknowns in this comparison sit in other principles, not in source.",
"RMK3-006": "Source is the one principle that is the same for humans and aquatic wildlife. Flushing, animal waste and human waste reach the water both are exposed to. Every other principle differs. Wildlife are exposed for their entire life, repeatedly and constantly, while for humans exposure depends on how they are exposed and where they live. Dose shares only its first sentence, that the real dose is not known. Target and mechanism of action lists different unknowns for each, with only the multiple exposures gap in both. These differences are why she said humans are probably OK while wildlife are a concern.",
"RMK3-007": "The human routes are drinking water, eating fish, and recreational contact through the skin. Drinking water is the main one. She said most of our exposure is through drinking water; you could get exposed by bathing or by swimming in a lake, but usually for us it is drinking water. Fish live in the contaminated water, so eating them is a second ingestion route. The trap is antibiotic-resistant bacteria, which came up as a separate concern, not a route. A prescribed dose of a drug is intended use, not exposure to drugs in water.",
"RMK3-008": "Aquatic wildlife are exposed by drinking the water, by eating food, and through the skin. They live in the contaminated water, so everything they do there is a route. She said they get exposed drinking, eating, swimming in the water, and the food includes other animals that have been living in that water. Frogs were the example for the skin route. The trap is a prescribed or veterinary dose. Wild animals are not given these drugs; they are the ones being exposed, not the source.",
"RMK3-009": "For humans, the duration, the single versus repeated nature, and the frequency of exposure all depend on how people are exposed and where they live. Human exposure varies from person to person: whether someone drinks from a contaminated source, eats fish from it or swims in it, and where they live. So one answer covers all three. The trap is the fixed wildlife answers. Aquatic wildlife live in the water, so their exposure lasts their entire life and is constant if their home range is continually contaminated. A lifelong daily intake is not the human answer either.",
"RMK3-010": "For aquatic wildlife, exposure lasts their entire life. They live in the contaminated water, so they never leave the exposure behind. The same fact makes their exposure repeated, and constant if their home range is continually contaminated. The trap is the human answer, that exposure depends on how they are exposed and where they live. Exposure limited to a breeding season does not fit animals that live in the water all the time, and a single exposure contradicts the repeated exposure listed for them.",
"RMK3-011": "Aquatic wildlife exposure is repeated. They do not visit the water; they live in it. Every time they drink, eat other animals living there, or swim, they are exposed again, for their entire life. The exposure is also constant if their home range is continually contaminated. The trap is a single exposure, which fits a one-time event, not an animal living in contaminated water. Depending on how they are exposed is the human answer, and this entry is not left unknown; the answer given is repeated.",
"RMK3-012": "For aquatic wildlife, the frequency of exposure is constant, if their home range is continually contaminated. The entry adds that contamination levels may vary even in one location. In class she agreed that animals living in or near water all the time are exposed constantly. The trap is dropping that caveat and saying the level is the same in every part of the range. Depending on how people are exposed and where they live is the human answer, and occasional exposure does not fit animals living in the water.",
"RMK3-013": "The contamination level may vary even in one location. The wildlife frequency entry says exposure is constant if the home range is continually contaminated, and then adds that levels may vary even in one location. Constant exposure does not mean a constant level. The trap is reading constant exposure as one uniform level across the home range. Nothing says the level is above a therapeutic dose; the real dose is not known. Nothing says levels drop to zero either, since current treatment does not get all the drugs out.",
"RMK3-014": "Aquatic wildlife exposure is the more significant. Wildlife are exposed for their entire life, repeatedly, and constantly if their home range is contaminated. For humans, the dose is probably below a therapeutic dose, and the exposure depends on how and where they live. She said your patient is probably OK; we are worried about wildlife. The trap is choosing humans, or calling the two equal. Another trap says parts per trillion is too low to matter, but for wildlife the dose is likely high enough to cause effects because of the nature of their exposure.",
"RMK3-016": "For humans, there are some data from drug development studies, and perhaps from other studies. Developing a drug establishes how it is absorbed, distributed, metabolized and excreted in people, along with its dose and mechanism. The gap is the metabolites. She said a lot of the medications we ingest get metabolized to substances we do not know anything about; we do not even know what the metabolites are. The trap is the wildlife answer, very little if any data. Complete data on every metabolite is wrong, and these drugs do not all leave the body unchanged.",
"RMK3-017": "For aquatic wildlife, there is very little if any data on whether these drugs are metabolized to anything harmful, because the studies have not been done. Drug development tests rodents, guinea pigs, dogs and sometimes pigs, not fish or mollusks. She summed it up: we know it all for humans, except for the metabolites, and we do not know it for these critters out there. The trap is the human answer, some data from drug development studies. Saying the drugs do or do not form harmful metabolites in fish claims a result that has not been studied.",
"RMK3-018": "There are so few wildlife data because drug development uses species such as rodents, guinea pigs, dogs and sometimes pigs. She said we do not tend to use sea otters and orca whales and fish and mollusks in drug development studies, so we do not know how these drugs made for humans and tested for humans are going to act. The studies have not been done. The trap is assuming fish do not absorb drugs, when they are exposed through water, food and skin. The data are not held back by companies, and wildlife are not assumed to match humans.",
"RMK3-019": "For both humans and aquatic wildlife, we do not know the dose they are really exposed to. Many values have not been measured, are undetected, or are modeled. That sentence opens both columns of the dose principle. The conclusions after it differ. For humans, the dose is probably below the therapeutic dose. For wildlife, the doses that cause adverse effects are unknown, but their dose is likely high enough to cause effects. The trap is claiming the dose is known from monitoring, equal to a therapeutic dose, or the same for both.",
"RMK3-020": "Three reasons leave the real dose unknown: many values have not been measured, many are undetected, and many are modeled. A dose estimate needs a measured concentration in the water. Without a measurement there is no number. An undetected value gives no concentration to work from. A modeled value is an estimate, not a measurement, so a dose built on it is uncertain. The trap is saying the concentrations are too high to measure. In class she said the concentrations in drinking water were usually parts per trillion, which is low, not high.",
"RMK3-021": "For humans, the dose from drinking water is probably below the therapeutic dose of a pharmaceutical. Most of these drugs are in drinking water at parts per trillion. She asked whether that concentration is going to get you up to a normal therapeutic daily dose, and answered no. Even a person who drinks a lot of water does not reach it. That is why she said the worried patient is probably OK. The trap is the wildlife conclusion, a dose likely high enough to cause effects. A dose equal to one therapeutic dose a day is also wrong.",
"RMK3-022": "For aquatic wildlife, the doses needed to cause adverse effects are not known, but because of the nature of their exposure, their dose is likely high enough to cause effects. Their exposure lasts their entire life, is repeated, and is constant where the home range is contaminated. She said that for these animals we do not know their absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, the dose that will cause a problem, or where the drug will cause a problem in their bodies. The trap is the human answer, below a therapeutic dose. No no-effect dose is known for them.",
"RMK3-023": "For humans, the dose is probably below a therapeutic dose; for aquatic wildlife, it is likely high enough to cause effects. Both begin from the same sentence, that the real exposure dose is not known. Humans meet these drugs mostly in drinking water at parts per trillion, which does not reach a therapeutic dose. Wildlife are exposed for their entire life, repeatedly and constantly. The trap is swapping the two conclusions. Saying both are below therapeutic is wrong, and saying the dose is known and measured contradicts the shared first sentence.",
"RMK3-024": "Three gaps are listed for humans: the effects of low-dose chronic exposures, good data on sensitive populations, and how multiple exposures affect the target and mechanism of action. Human exposure from water is low but goes on over time, and what that does is not known. Sensitive populations are the fetus, children, the elderly and people with underlying disease states. Water carries many drugs at once, and their interactions are very hard to predict. The trap is the target of each parent drug, which drug development established. Measuring in the correct species is a wildlife gap.",
"RMK3-025": "The sensitive populations lacking good data are the fetus, children, the elderly and people with underlying disease states. She said not to ignore our sensitive populations, which are in utero, the elderly, kids and people with diseases. Risk assessment on these groups is very hard to do, so good data are lacking. The trap is the healthy adult who drinks a lot of water, who was her example of someone who still does not reach a therapeutic dose. Fish and mollusks are aquatic wildlife, the other column, not a sensitive human population.",
"RMK3-026": "Four gaps are listed for wildlife: the system or organ affected is not always known, the data may not have been measured in the correct species, studies may not have measured the correct endpoint, and how multiple exposures affect the target and mechanism of action is not known. Drugs are made for humans and tested in species such as rodents and dogs, not fish or mollusks. She said it is hard to study all these animals. The trap is the human gaps: data on the fetus, children and elderly, and the effects of low-dose chronic exposures.",
"RMK3-027": "How multiple exposures affect the target and mechanism of action is the gap shared by humans and wildlife. Contaminated water carries many drugs at once, so both are exposed to several compounds together. Those compounds can interact additively, synergistically, by potentiation or antagonistically, which is very hard to predict or quantify. The other gaps belong to one column only. Measuring in the correct species and measuring the correct endpoint are wildlife gaps. The effects of low-dose chronic exposures is a human gap.",
"RMK3-028": "The gap is that the data may not have been measured in the correct species. Drug development studies use rodents, guinea pigs, dogs and pigs, not fish, so how a drug made and tested for humans acts in fish is not known. A result from a laboratory species may not apply to fish. A related wildlife gap is that studies may not have measured the correct endpoint. The trap is a human gap, such as sensitive populations or low-dose chronic exposures. Below a therapeutic dose is the human dose conclusion, not a gap about target and mechanism.",
"RMK3-029": "Sara Rodriguez-Mozaz and Howard Weinberg, of the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, wrote the article. It is a meeting report titled Pharmaceuticals in Water, An Interdisciplinary Approach to a Public Health Challenge, published in Environmental Health Perspectives. Her discussion guide applies the principles of toxicology to it: source, exposure, absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, dose, and target. The trap is the authors of the other readings. Marraffa, Cohen and Howland wrote the antidotes primer, and Wax and Olson are the first authors of the two poison center guidelines, written by a panel that includes Erdman, Chyka and Woolf.",
"RMK3-030": "The article is a meeting report. It summarizes the discussions at an environmental health summit in North Carolina, where government agencies, academics, industry, water utilities and public interest groups discussed drugs found in water. The authors write that they present an overview of the summit discussions. That is why its conclusions are framed as research needs and recommendations, not trial results. The trap is a clinical trial; no one was dosed. A consensus guideline for poison centers describes the two overdose guidelines, and a review of antidotes is the Marraffa primer."
});
